From: Stained Glass OS contributors
Subject: network shares with a name and password: net use /user, Enter network credentials

A Windows PC's shares refuse a guest (Windows 10 and 11 set them so), and
Stained Glass OS mounted shares with a Kerberos ticket or as a guest only:
File Explorer listed such a PC's shares with no files in them.
sg-netmountd's LOGON mounts a share with a name and password for one user,
under /run/stained-glass-net/users/<uid>/unc/<server>/<share>. Here:

- ntdll resolves \\server\share to the user's own connection first;
- ntlanman's NPAddConnection3 with a name sends LOGON (the password never
  in a trace), then MOUNT or MAP as before; cancelling a UNC connection
  sends LOGOFF;
- net use takes [password | *] and /USER:[domain\]name, as Windows' does
  ("*" asks for the password without echoing it);
- File Explorer's Network folder asks "Enter network credentials"
  (credui) when a share will not open -- parsing \\server\share (the
  address bar, opening it from the computer's folder) or binding to it --
  and connects with what is typed (WNetAddConnection2), three tries; a
  Cancel is remembered for 30 s.

